const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Traverse's own repo is a workspace of eight Rust crates. Each one has a specific job. You can use them together through the CLI or pull individual crates into your own code.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-runtime</h2>\n      <p>The core execution engine. It drives the state machine from <code>idle</code> through discovery, constraint evaluation, selection, and execution. Every placement target builds on this crate. You use it if you are embedding Traverse in a Rust application rather than calling it from the CLI.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-contracts</h2>\n      <p>Defines the contract schema: input and output types, preconditions, postconditions, placement constraints, and the governing spec. The runtime uses this crate to parse, validate, and enforce contracts. You interact with it by writing TOML contract files.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-cli</h2>\n      <p>The <code>traverse</code> command you install with <code>cargo install traverse-cli</code>. It wraps the runtime and MCP server in a terminal interface. Most developers only need this crate directly.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-mcp</h2>\n      <p>Implements the MCP stdio server that exposes your capabilities to AI agents. It supports <code>tools/list</code> and <code>tools/call</code>. It ships as its own binary, started with <code>traverse-mcp stdio</code>. See <a href=\"/questions/how-does-the-mcp-server-work.html\">how the MCP server works</a> for details.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-embedder</h2>\n      <p>The public Rust embedder SDK for host applications — Linux GTK apps and CLI tools — that want to load and run an application-owned bundle of capabilities directly, without going through the CLI's server process.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-native-bridge and traverse-swift-host</h2>\n      <p>The building blocks for platform-native mobile embedders. <code>traverse-native-bridge</code> is a deterministic builder for the governed WebAssembly runtime artifact those bridges load. <code>traverse-swift-host</code> is the Apple static-library host — packaged as the <code>TraverseSwiftHost</code> XCFramework — behind the Swift/iOS runtime bridge.</p>\n\n      <h2>traverse-expedition-wasm</h2>\n      <p>Compiles the bundled \"expedition\" example domain to <code>wasm32-wasi</code> for testing and demos. It is not the browser embedder — browser execution is handled separately by the public Web/TypeScript embedder SDK, which runs directly against the browser's native WebAssembly host.</p>\n\n      <h2>What about traverse-registry?</h2>\n      <p>It used to live in this workspace, but it was extracted into its own repository, <code>traverse-framework/registry</code>, and is now consumed here as an independently versioned, externally published dependency rather than a workspace crate.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What crates make up Traverse?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Traverse's repo is made up of eight crates: traverse-runtime (core execution engine), traverse-contracts (contract schema and validation), traverse-cli (command-line interface), traverse-mcp (MCP stdio server for AI agents), traverse-embedder (public Rust embedder SDK), traverse-native-bridge and traverse-swift-host (native mobile bridge building blocks), and traverse-expedition-wasm (the bundled example domain compiled to wasm32-wasi). The capability registry, traverse-registry, was extracted into its own repository and is now an external published dependency, not a workspace crate.\"}}]}";
